module sysl.env.sys

// The calls `sysl.env` makes of whatever holds the environment underneath it: what one name is set
// to, how many variables there are, and the one at a given place.
//
// **Each is an `extern` naming a symbol no module here defines**, so what answers it is decided by
// what the program is linked with (`reference/ffi.md § A module may supply another module's extern`),
// exactly as for `sysl.fs.sys`, `sysl.io.sys`, `sysl.process.sys` and `sysl.net.sys`:
//
// | linked with | what defines the symbols |
// |---|---|
// | a program for a hosted target | `sysl.env.hosted`, over `getenv` and, where there is POSIX, `environ` |
// | a program for a target with no C library | the program, with an `@export` per hook it reaches |
//
// A freestanding program that reaches one of these and leaves it unanswered is refused when it is
// compiled, naming the hooks it reached, rather than at the link -- where it would have named C's
// `getenv` and `environ`, which no line of the program mentions.
//
// ## The shape of a hook
//
// **A hook answers one integer: what it did, or what went wrong** -- the contract of every `*.sys`
// module. Zero or more is success: a count for `count`, and for `get` and `entry` one where there is
// something to write and zero where there is not -- a name that is not set, a place past the end. A
// negative answer is the code of the failure negated, and **a target that cannot perform a call at all
// answers `UNSUPPORTED`** (`sysl.sys`, -38 on every platform) -- which is what a hosted target with no
// POSIX answers for `count` and `entry`, C alone having no way to list the names.
//
// **Text crosses as a pointer and a length, never as a C string.** A name is lent as two parameters,
// and a value or an entry is written into the caller's `room` bytes at `into`, with its whole length
// written into `got` -- **the whole length, even where it is more than `room`**, in which case only the
// first `room` bytes are written and the caller asks again with more. Nothing is terminated by a zero.

/** What `name` (`name_len` bytes, lent) is set to: one where it is set, its value's bytes written
 * into `into` and the value's whole length into `got`; zero where it is not set, and `got` untouched.
 * Set-to-empty is one with a length of zero.
 */
private[sysl] extern "sysl_env_get" get(name: *u8, name_len: usize, into: *u8, room: usize,
                                        got: *usize) -> int

/** How many variables the environment holds. */
private[sysl] extern "sysl_env_count" count() -> isize

/** The variable at place `i`, counting from zero in the order the environment holds them, as the
 * `NAME=value` bytes C keeps it as: one, written into `into` and its whole length into `got`; zero
 * where there is no variable at that place, and `got` untouched. The library asks for the places below
 * what `count` answered.
 */
private[sysl] extern "sysl_env_entry" entry(i: usize, into: *u8, room: usize, got: *usize) -> int
